  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1241 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nTomorrow, an 80kt 500mb jet will move over the area ahead of the \napproaching shortwave. At 700mb, models have winds around 35kts \nto 55kts. Conditions do not look favorable for mountain wave \namplification in our area due to the flow being more southwesterly and\na few other factors. However, we are expecting strong winds to be\nforced/mixed down to the surface in the late morning through the \nafternoon. Wind gusts across the plains will be around 30 to 50mph\nin the afternoon. The one exception will be the northern I-25 \ncorridor and portions of the northern plains, which tend to remain\nmore sheltered from the strong winds in southwest flow. In the \nhigher elevations, wind gusts will near 50 to 65mph in the late \nmorning. A front will approach our northern counties late Friday \nafternoon, turning winds more towards the west/northwest in our \nnorthern counties. This will allow for stronger winds to finally \nmake their way into the northern I-25 corridor and northern \nplains, with gusts around 25mph to 40mph. There will be elevated \nfire weather conditions Friday afternoon due to the winds, but \nrelative humidities are forecast to remain above critical \nthresholds. The front will move through the area overnight, \nbringing cooler temperatures for Saturday.\n\nThe approaching shortwave, weak orographics, and weak surface \nconvergence could be enough to trigger a few showers in the \nhigher terrain and plains overnight Friday into the late morning\nSaturday. Moisture will be rather limited, so snow accumulations \nwill remain on the low end. With the exception of the Park Range, \nwhich should see slightly higher amounts, snow accumulations will \ngenerally be a few hundredths to about 3 inches in the higher \nterrain. Chances for at least a trace of snow in the metro remains\non the low end (~20% chance or less). Temperatures will likely \nsupport at least a rain/snow mix if not all snow Saturday morning \nacross the plains, it's just the lack of moisture and lack of a \nstrong forcing that's inhibiting snow chances across the plains.\n\nCooler temperatures will continue across the forecast area \nthrough this forecast. Low temperatures Sunday through the middle\nof next week will be in the teens to 20s. Our next system will \napproach Colorado on Monday, with a shortwave moving through late \nMonday into Tuesday. The timing and positioning of the shortwave \nvaries between models, lowering our confidence in precip chances \nwith this system. Right now, we expect light snow showers in the \nmountains with a medium chance (30% to 50%) that snow showers make\nit onto the plains. If we do end up with some snow over the \nplains, accumulations will be light and impacts are not \nanticipated. The active pattern is expected to continue into later \nnext week, but models continue to swing back and forth with the \npotential impacts and confidence in any one model solution is very\nlow at this time.",
